Most often, it's one of three things:

**Your parent re-renders.** When a parent renders, all its children render too—unless you stop them. That's the default behavior.

**Your props are being remade.** If you're passing an object, array, or function created fresh on each render—like `<Child data={{ id: 1 }} />` or `<Child onClick={() => handle()} />`—React sees it as a new prop every time, even if the value is the same. React compares by reference, not value.

**Your child isn't memoized.** Wrap it with `React.memo` to tell React: only re-render if the props actually changed.

```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ value, onClick }) => {
  return <div onClick={onClick}>{value}</div>;
});
```

To fix the recreated props, pull functions and objects out of the render:

```jsx
// Before: onClick recreated every render
<Child onClick={() => handle()} />

// After: same function each time
const handleClick = () => { /* ... */ };
<Child onClick={handleClick} />
```

Or use `useCallback` for functions and `useMemo` for objects if they need to depend on state:

```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
  // uses current props/state
}, [dependency]);
```

Check your browser DevTools Profiler—it shows exactly which components are re-rendering and why. That's the quickest way to spot which of these is your culprit.
